  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday) \nIssued at 1035 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThe aforementioned storms should be quite fast moving with subsequent \nfast steering flow. The dryline looks to move completely east of the \nforecast area by the afternoon hours. If it slows down at all, a few \nstorms could develop off the far eastern Caprock. The main concern \nhowever for Monday afternoon remains the breezy westerly winds. \nThere are two closed lows that will develop in the lee of the \nRockies. The stronger of which will be well to the north, reaching \nSouth Dakota. The southern low is slightly more disorganized and not \nas compact. Thus it is not the best setup for the very strong wind \nevents we see here mostly in the winter. That said, winds aloft will \nbe favorable to mix down with surface gusts potentially reaching 45 \nmph. Winds will diminish overnight into Tuesday and gradually turn \nmore northerly behind a surface cold front. Highs Tuesday will fall \ninto the low-to-mid 60s although the cooler dry air will allow for \nskies to clear. A few weak disturbances will move around our area \nthrough the remainder of the week, however no precipitation chances \nare expected at this time. Highs will remain slightly below average, \ngenerally in the mid 60s.",
